## Schema

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id          bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  table_name  text NOT NULL,
  row_id      text NOT NULL,
  action      text NOT NULL CHECK (action IN ('INSERT','UPDATE','DELETE')),
  actor_id    uuid,              -- app user, nullable for system/unattributed writes
  actor_email text,
  db_role     text NOT NULL,     -- session_user fallback, always populated
  old_values  jsonb,
  new_values  jsonb,
  request_id  text,
  changed_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
) PARTITION BY RANGE (changed_at);
```

Partition by month so old partitions can be shipped to cold storage instead of deleted — compliance retention (SOX-style 7 years, etc.) becomes an archival job, not a `DELETE`.

## Capture mechanism

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION audit_row() RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO audit_log(table_name, row_id, action, actor_id, actor_email, db_role, old_values, new_values, request_id)
  VALUES (
    TG_TABLE_NAME,
    COALESCE(NEW.id, OLD.id)::text,
    TG_OP,
    NULLIF(current_setting('app.actor_id', true), '')::uuid,
    NULLIF(current_setting('app.actor_email', true), ''),
    session_user,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'INSERT' THEN to_jsonb(OLD) END,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'DELETE' THEN to_jsonb(NEW) END,
    NULLIF(current_setting('app.request_id', true), '')
  );
  RETURN COALESCE(NEW, OLD);
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql SECURITY DEFINER;

CREATE TRIGGER accounts_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON accounts
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
CREATE TRIGGER orders_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON orders
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
```

`session_user` is always set, so even a raw `psql` update from an engineer gets attributed to *some* identity even with `app.actor_id` unset — no silent gaps.

