**Frontend**: debounce the input (~250ms), call the endpoint, show a no-results state. Nothing exotic needed for "type a few words, see matches."

One tradeoff worth flagging: `tsvector` matches on whole words/stems, not substrings — a typo or partial word (`"shrt sleve"`) won't match `"short sleeve"`. If that turns out to matter, `pg_trgm` with a trigram GIN index on `name` bolts on typo-tolerant/prefix matching later. I'd ship plain full-text search first and only add that if users actually hit the gap.

One client hammering `/v1` degrades everyone because of `db.js:3` — a single `pg.Pool()` per worker process, with 4 workers × N boxes all sharing one Postgres. The noisy client isn't just using its own capacity, it's queueing up connections that every other client's `/v1/reports` call also needs. Fixing this means stopping the offender before it reaches the pool, not just logging it after.

**Key by client, not IP.** `routes/api.js:9-19` already resolves `X-Api-Key` → `client_id` before anything else runs — and the comment above it says why IP won't work here: clients share NAT, and one client calls from many addresses. Rate limit on `req.client.client_id`.

**Where:** a middleware inserted right after the auth lookup and before the route handlers, so a client that's over budget gets rejected before touching Postgres at all — that's the point where you actually protect the pool.

**Store:** Redis, since it's already the one piece of state shared across every worker on every box (`redis.js:3-4` — same reasoning that makes it the session store applies here). In-process counters won't work; each of the 4 workers has its own heap.

**Algorithm:** token bucket / GCRA over fixed windows — fixed windows let a client burst 2x at the window boundary, and with a hammering client that matters. Implement it as a single Redis Lua script (`EVAL`) so the check-and-increment is atomic in one round trip; with 4 workers × N boxes hitting Redis concurrently, a get-then-set from application code races.

Rough shape:

```lua
-- KEYS[1] = rate:{client_id}, ARGV = now, rate, capacity
local bucket = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts')
-- refill based on elapsed time, reject if tokens < 1, else decrement and allow
```

Wrapped by middleware:

```js
router.use(async (req, res, next) => {
  const { allowed, retryAfter } = await checkLimit(req.client.client_id, req.client.plan);
  if (!allowed) {
    res.set('Retry-After', retryAfter);
    return res.status(429).end();
  }
  next();
});
```

**Limits per plan:** `req.client.plan` is already fetched in the same query at `routes/api.js:12-15`. Add rate/burst columns to `api_keys` (or a small config map keyed by plan) so the limit comes back in that same row — no extra query.

**Response contract:** `429` + `Retry-After` + `RateLimit-Limit`/`RateLimit-Remaining` headers, so a well-behaved client's SDK can back off instead of retrying immediately and making it worse.

**Failure mode:** decide explicitly what happens if Redis errors — fail-open (allow, log, alert) is usually right here, since Redis going down shouldn't take down all `/v1` traffic on top of already being the session store's single point of failure.

**Defense in depth:** the rate limit caps requests per client over time, but a client could still stay under it while holding many requests in flight at once. Since the actual failure mode is pool exhaustion, it's worth also bounding concurrent in-flight requests per client (or just sizing the Postgres pool and tuning the rate limit so worst-case concurrent clients can't collectively exceed it) — the rate limiter alone doesn't guarantee the pool stays healthy, it just makes exhausting it much harder for any single client.

